WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:06.323
Three sodium channels sit in a nociceptor.

2
00:00:06.983 --> 00:00:08.123
Which of them builds the spike?

3
00:00:09.183 --> 00:00:13.343
Nociceptor spikes draw on three channels concentrated in these neurons.

4
00:00:14.383 --> 00:00:18.403
Two of them are Nav1.7 and Nav1.8.

5
00:00:19.223 --> 00:00:21.063
The third is Nav1.9.

6
00:00:22.033 --> 00:00:24.473
The last two are tetrodotoxin resistant.

7
00:00:25.493 --> 00:00:28.053
That is how their currents are isolated in a recording.

8
00:00:29.117 --> 00:00:34.037
Those two carry pore residues that weaken the toxin's grip, so the toxin leaves them

9
00:00:34.037 --> 00:00:35.037
conducting.

10
00:00:35.567 --> 00:00:40.427
and a V1 .7 and N, a V1 .8 contribute to the spike itself.

11
00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:47.220
Nav1.9 mainly sets the resting potential and subthreshold excitability.

12
00:00:48.383 --> 00:00:50.143
Keep two kinds of current apart.

13
00:00:50.983 --> 00:00:54.523
A transient current rises and then inactivates within milliseconds.

14
00:00:55.683 --> 00:00:59.463
A persistent current stays appreciable while the membrane sits still.

15
00:01:00.343 --> 00:01:03.483
Its inactivation is slow or incomplete, not absent.

16
00:01:04.517 --> 00:01:07.837
Nav1.9 supplies a persistent current.

17
00:01:08.677 --> 00:01:12.037
Its slow gating suits a role at rest rather than in the upstroke.

18
00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:15.410
Now the quantity this lesson turns on.

19
00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:20.410
Availability is the fraction of sodium channels not already inactivated.

20
00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:26.480
Channels sitting in the inactivated state cannot open when the membrane depolarizes.

21
00:01:27.533 --> 00:01:31.993
Availability depends on the voltage the membrane has been resting at.

22
00:01:32.653 --> 00:01:36.193
Hold one voltage long enough and availability settles on this curve.

23
00:01:37.183 --> 00:01:40.743
The curve has a half-inactivation voltage and a slope factor.

24
00:01:41.603 --> 00:01:46.243
For Nav1.7-like channels the slope is about seven millivolts.

25
00:01:47.023 --> 00:01:51.523
Other subtypes, especially Nav1.8, need their own measured curves.

26
00:01:52.517 --> 00:01:53.937
The curve runs downhill.

27
00:01:54.817 --> 00:01:58.317
The more depolarized the rest, the fewer channels are left to open.

28
00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:01.530
Two nociceptors carry the same channels.

29
00:02:02.410 --> 00:02:04.190
One rests low, one rests high.

30
00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:06.090
Which has more available?

31
00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:17.190
The low one. About two-thirds of those channels are available, against about

32
00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:18.190
one-fifth.

33
00:02:18.683 --> 00:02:22.723
Now give each neuron the same small depolarization, and hold it there.

34
00:02:23.523 --> 00:02:26.263
The low one keeps about three-quarters of its availability.

35
00:02:27.467 --> 00:02:29.047
The high one keeps about half.

36
00:02:29.927 --> 00:02:33.867
It was already low on the curve, so the same step costs it proportionally more.

37
00:02:34.867 --> 00:02:36.707
Those midpoints are illustrative.

38
00:02:36.707 --> 00:02:40.747
For a real neuron the midpoint is a measurement, not an assumption.

39
00:02:41.767 --> 00:02:44.867
So depolarization does two opposite things at once.

40
00:02:45.447 --> 00:02:48.607
It closes the gap to threshold, and it costs availability.

41
00:02:49.817 --> 00:02:51.677
That is the order to ask in.

42
00:02:52.477 --> 00:02:56.237
What happened to the channel, then to rest and availability, then to firing.

43
00:02:57.367 --> 00:03:00.627
Treat availability as a budget, but a reversible one.

44
00:03:01.587 --> 00:03:05.367
Channels recover from inactivation when the membrane goes negative again.

45
00:03:06.247 --> 00:03:09.287
The budget shows what a step costs, not whether the neuron fires.
